    "instruction": "I've been reading about magnesium glycinate, L-theanine, and ashwagandha for my late-night wakeups. Before you write me up a summary of which ones I should actually consider, confirm whether these candidates are backed by current evidence as relevant to my specific sleep-disruption pattern based on my recent late-night device activity and the available sleep-disruption evidence, then report which candidates are supported.",
    "system_prompt": "You are a digital wellness assistant helping a user review sleep-related habits and supplement research.",
    "final_output": "The verification gate has returned authoritative judgments. Here's your full evidence-backed report:\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 Your Sleep-Disruption Pattern (From Device Activity)\n\nYour late-night device log revealed a clear pattern:\n- **10:30–11:45 PM** — 75 min of **short-form video** (high-stimulation, rapid-cut content)\n- **11:50 PM–12:40 AM** — 50 min of **long-form video** (sustained engagement past midnight)\n\nThis pattern is consistent with **blue light-induced melatonin suppression**, **cortisol/alertness spikes** from engaging content, and **delayed sleep onset** — all of which are established contributors to mid-night wakeups.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Verification Results: All Three Candidates Are Supported\n\nThe verification gate confirmed each supplement as relevant to *your specific pattern*:\n\n---\n\n### 1. 🟢 Magnesium Glycinate — **Supported**\n- **Why it fits:** Magnesium is a co-factor in GABA production and helps regulate the nervous system's stress response. Late-night screen stimulation elevates cortisol and sympathetic nervous system activity; magnesium glycinate may help counter this arousal, supporting deeper, more continuous sleep.\n- **Evidence anchor:** Matched to your search `\"magnesium glycinate sleep benefits\"` and your pattern of cortisol-disrupted sleep.\n\n---\n\n### 2. 🟢 L-Theanine — **Supported**\n- **Why it fits:** L-theanine promotes alpha-wave brain activity (calm alertness → relaxation) without sedation. It's particularly well-suited for people who wake mid-night in an alert or anxious state — precisely what overstimulated late-night screen use can cause.\n- **Evidence anchor:** Matched to your search `\"L-theanine for nighttime waking\"` and your profile of stimulation-driven wakeups.\n\n---\n\n### 3. 🟢 Ashwagandha — **Supported**\n- **Why it fits:** Ashwagandha (KSM-66 extract in particular) has evidence for reducing cortisol levels and improving sleep quality in stressed individuals. Given your late-night high-engagement content use, elevated stress hormones are a likely mechanism behind your wakeups.\n- **Evidence anchor:** Matched to your search `\"ashwagandha sleep evidence\"` and the cortisol-stimulation component of your pattern.\n\n---\n\n## 🧭 Summary Table\n\n| Supplement | Supported? | Primary Mechanism for Your Pattern |\n|---|---|---|\n| Magnesium Glycinate | ✅ Yes | GABA support, nervous system calm, cortisol regulation |\n| L-Theanine | ✅ Yes | Reduces mid-night alertness, promotes relaxation |\n| Ashwagandha | ✅ Yes | Cortisol reduction, HPA-axis regulation |\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ A Note on Root Cause\n\nAll three are legitimately supported — but the data also shows a clear **behavioral contributor**: 125 minutes of stimulating video content ending after midnight. Supplements can blunt the downstream effects, but **reducing screen time before bed** (especially short-form, high-stimulation video) would address the root cause directly. Consider pairing these supplements with a device wind-down cutoff around 10 PM for best results.\n\n*As always, consult your healthcare provider before starting any new supplement regimen.*",
